+  {
+    "lesson": 4,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n10\">The <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A small cylindrical or round box with a lid, used to store precious items such as jewellery; in Mycenaean contexts, gold pyxides were prestige objects placed in elite burials.\">gold pyxis</dfn> from Shaft Grave V in Grave Circle A is a small round box with a fitted lid, hammered from gold sheet and decorated with running spirals in repousse &mdash; a technique where the design is pushed outward from the inside of the metal sheet to create raised relief. Running spirals are one of the most characteristic Mycenaean decorative motifs. The combination of gold material, fine craftsmanship and a decorative scheme shared across Mycenaean culture suggests that the pyxis was a prestige object associated with high social status, possibly used to store cosmetics, oils or jewellery.</p>",
