+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n13\">Not all Mycenaean decorated objects were made of precious metals. The <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A large painted storage jar (krater) from the House of the Warrior at Mycenae, dated to around 1200 BC, showing a line of soldiers marching with spears, shields and crested helmets.\">Mycenaean warrior vase</dfn> is a krater (a large mixing vessel for wine) found in the House of the Warrior at Mycenae, dated to around 1200 BC &mdash; near the end of the Mycenaean period. Its decoration, painted in dark brown on a cream ground, shows two rows of marching soldiers facing each other. The soldiers carry long spears, round shields painted with geometric patterns, and wear crested helmets. They are clearly dressed for war, not ceremony.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n13\">Not all Mycenaean decorated objects were made of precious metals. The <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A large painted storage jar (krater) from the House of the Warrior at Mycenae, dated to around 1200 BC, showing a line of soldiers marching with spears, shields and crested helmets.\">Mycenaean warrior vase</dfn> is a krater (a large mixing vessel for wine) found in the House of the Warrior at Mycenae, dated to around 1200 BC &mdash; near the end of the Mycenaean period. Its decoration presents two linked scenes. On one side, soldiers march in full battle gear while a woman raises her hands in farewell. On the other, soldiers attack with raised spears.</p>",
